{
  "slug": "sneaker-resale-shipping-day",
  "title": "Sneaker Resale With a Shipping Day",
  "topic": "dynamic-programming",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "pattern": "dynamic programming",
  "aka": "Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Cooldown",
  "order": 13,
  "story": [
    "<p>You resell one popular sneaker model from a small flat. There is room for only <strong>one pair at a time</strong>. A price tracker gives you a good forecast of the price for each of the next few days, and you can buy or sell at that day's price.</p>",
    "<p>Every sale has to be packed and taken to the post office the next day, which eats the whole day. So on the day right after a sale you cannot buy. You can repeat buy-and-sell rounds as often as you like. How much can you make?</p>"
  ],
  "task": [
    "<p>Write <code>maxProfit(prices)</code>. <code>prices[d]</code> is the price on day <code>d</code>. Each day you do one thing: buy a pair (only if you hold none), sell your pair (only if you hold one), or wait. After a sale on day <code>d</code>, you may not buy on day <code>d + 1</code>.</p>",
    "<p>Return the largest total profit (money from sales minus money spent on buying). Ending with a pair still unsold only loses money, so the best plan never does it. If no trade makes money, return 0.</p>"
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "0 ≤ prices.length ≤ 5,000",
    "0 ≤ prices[d] ≤ 1,000"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "args": [[4, 7, 2, 9, 1, 6]],
      "expected": 8,
      "explanation": "Buy at 4, sell at 7 (+3). Day 2 is the shipping day. Wait on day 3. Buy at 1, sell at 6 (+5). Total 8. The biggest single jump, 2 to 9, earns 7, but selling on day 3 makes day 4 a shipping day, so you miss the 1 and end with 7."
    },
    {
      "args": [[3, 5, 2, 6]],
      "expected": 4,
      "explanation": "Without the shipping day you could earn 2 + 4 = 6. But selling at 5 on day 1 blocks buying at 2 on day 2. The best is one trade: buy at 2, sell at 6."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "At the end of any day, what situations can you be in? Try listing them for the second example. Is \"I have no pair\" enough information to decide what you may do tomorrow?",
    "No: an empty-handed day right after a sale is different from an ordinary empty-handed day, because tomorrow you may not buy. So there are three situations: <strong>holding</strong> a pair, <strong>just sold</strong> today, and <strong>free</strong> (no pair, allowed to buy tomorrow).",
    "Track the best profit so far for each of the three situations. How can you get into each one today? Holding: you held yesterday, or you were free yesterday and buy today. Just sold: you held yesterday and sell today. Free: you were free yesterday, or you sold yesterday (today is the shipping day).",
    "Walk the days once, updating three numbers from yesterday's three: <code>hold = max(hold, free - price)</code>, <code>sold = holdBefore + price</code>, <code>free = max(free, soldBefore)</code>. Start with <code>hold = -infinity</code> and the others at 0. Compute all three from the old values before overwriting. The answer is the larger of <code>sold</code> and <code>free</code>."
  ],
  "bruteForce": [
    "<p>Try every plan: each day buy, sell or wait, where the rules allow. That is up to 3ⁿ plans; for 30 days it is already about 2 × 10¹⁴, and the limit is 5,000 days.</p>",
    "<p>The well-known trick for unlimited trades, adding up every rise from one day to the next, ignores the shipping day. It gives 6 on the second example, where only 4 is possible.</p>"
  ],
